WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.533
- It's my pleasure to introduce
Dr. Sarah Walker here today.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.330
Dr. Walker is a senior lecturer
at the School of Economics

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:18.360
at the University of New South
Wales in Sydney, Australia.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.090
We're really delighted that
she has agreed to get up early

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.250
so she can have part of
her day overlap with ours

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.043
and present in our seminar this afternoon.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.850
Sarah is a really very
talented emerging scholar

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.070
in the fields of development economics,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.410
environmental economics, economic history.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.770
Sort of her work centers on
a lot of really interesting

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.410
questions related to
institutions, culture,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.993
and also sort of
development the environment.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.580
Sarah's work has won sort of
several honors and awards.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.904
So in 2020, the Paul Burke
Award for early career research

00:00:53.904 --> 00:00:57.150
from the Academy of Social
Sciences in Australia.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.170
She's also a winner

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.180
of the Discovery Early
Career Research Award

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.010
from the Australian Research Council.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.882
And today she's going to
be sharing some of her

00:01:04.882 --> 00:01:07.530
really fascinating work
with us that she's done

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.963
on refugee communities in Kenya.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.103
I will pass it over to Sarah for her talk.

00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:21.210
She is allowed interruptions throughout

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.800
sort of in the econ style talk.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.570
So if you have any questions,

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.460
please raise your hand
or use the chat feature.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.370
If you use the chat feature, I
will sort of find a nice time

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.050
to interject and ask the questions.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.020
That way she doesn't have
to monitor the chat herself.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.999
So without further ado,
I'll pass it over to Sarah

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:37.203
for the talk.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.510
- Great. Thank you so much.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:40.500
Thank you so much, Emily.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.884
Thank you so much to the
center for inviting me.

00:01:42.884 --> 00:01:44.910
This is a real pleasure,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.943
and I'm excited to share
this work with you.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.190
This is actually kind of an
older project at this point.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.220
This is first of all joint
work with Jennifer Alix-Garcia,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.070
who is an economist at
Oregon State University,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.760
and incidentally is also
the former PhD advisor

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.710
to myself and to Emily.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.225
So we share a very common bond there.

00:02:08.225 --> 00:02:10.265
And it's also joint
work with Anne Bartlett,

00:02:10.265 --> 00:02:13.680
who's a sociologist at
University of New South Wales

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:14.513
here in Sydney.

00:02:14.513 --> 00:02:19.513
So it's truly an
interdisciplinary set of work,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.899
and I'm delighted to share this

00:02:21.899 --> 00:02:25.290
with an interdisciplinary audience.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:30.290
So we actually collected the
data for this project in 2015,

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.580
and I'm going to present today
to you two papers actually

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.410
that have already been published.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:42.240
So one in 2018 and one in 2019,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.890
which is a little bit awkward, I must say,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.620
for an economist because we're
really used to presenting

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.830
work in progress and sort
of getting feedback there.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.160
But nonetheless, I think
it's important work

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.290
and I hope you do as well,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.900
and I'm still interested
obviously in your comments

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.060
and having a discussion around this.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.343
So thanks for that.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.770
So the motivation for
this work is, obviously,

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.320
it's no secret to the people
in this room that the numbers

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.770
of forcibly displaced people
are at an all-time high.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.950
So when you look at the
most recent data coming out

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.950
from UNHCR in 2022, this
stands at 103 million

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:26.340
displaced people, currently
32.5 million of whom

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.330
are refugees.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.580
And actually, as I was
preparing for this seminar,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.271
I went back to these slides
that I made a few years ago

00:03:32.271 --> 00:03:34.920
when it was still a work in progress.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.370
And at that time, the
data from UNHCR in 2016

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.400
was 65 million displaced people

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.730
and about 22.5 million refugees.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:49.200
So you can see even in these six years,

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.050
the numbers have skyrocketed
and they're only going

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.530
to continue to rise as
people flee affected regions

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.603
and seek asylum internationally.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.550
Something that I think we
also know about this situation

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.910
with refugees is that
many refugees are living

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.260
in permanent settlements.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.793
So they're living in camps.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.830
The best data that I could
come across is still from 2015,

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.090
when we were collecting our data.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.300
So about 41% of refugees
live in camps that have been

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.130
in place for over five years.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.490
These are long-term protracted situations,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.820
and there are seven settlements
worldwide with populations

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.410
over 80,000 people.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.540
So these are like cities,
these are small cities,

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.710
sometimes large cities,

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.540
depending on which camp you're looking at.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.100
So these camps are...

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.260
The largest camp is in Bangladesh

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.293
with over 700,000 refugees living there.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.290
We're going to look at a camp in Kenya.

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.720
Kenya has a couple of very large camps

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.710
with populations over 180,000 people.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.213
Other places include
Jordan, Uganda, and Sudan.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.680
So one of the main features
of the refugee situation

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.460
that I like to highlight
is that most refugees,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.280
83% of refugees are actually living

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.010
in low or middle-income countries.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:19.010
And in high-income countries
like the US or Australia,

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.140
where I'm living, or especially in Europe,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.500
a lot of the debate in
policy is coming around,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.480
is focused on, "Well,
how is this impacting us

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:28.740
and high-income countries?"

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.980
But one thing I like to
highlight is that the majority

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.790
of refugees are living in low
and middle-income countries

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.600
that already are not in the best position

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.430
to host refugees financially,
but nonetheless they do.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.550
The other statistic I'd like to highlight

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.480
is that a large percentage,
69% of refugees,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.310
come from just five countries.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:57.930
So, as we know, there's ongoing
crises in Syria, Venezuela,

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:02.580
Afghanistan, South Sudan,
Myanmar, which is only increasing.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.920
We are going to focus on the camp,

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.040
a particular camp in Kenya,
which is largely home

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.230
to South Sudanese
refugees who've been there

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.693
for now over 30 years.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:20.310
So the first question we
asked with this project was,

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:23.518
how do refugee camps
impact host communities?

00:06:23.518 --> 00:06:27.360
There's a large debate around
hosting displaced people.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.190
It's a very heated debate.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.560
But especially at the time
we were conducting this work,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.660
the empirical evidence was very sparse.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.070
I think in recent years there's
been a large contribution

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.040
to the literature, and I
think people in the room

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.410
probably know more about the
more recent work than I do,

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.290
and I'd love to hear what you have to say.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:49.530
But it's kind of an empirical question,

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.680
how do refugee camps
impact host communities?

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.290
And this is really important
for policymaking because,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.256
especially at the time we were
conducting our field work,

00:06:57.256 --> 00:07:00.120
the Kenya government was on the verge

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.320
of shutting down camps.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.250
And it was important to
understand what are the impacts

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.130
of these camps before
we throw the baby out

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.273
with the bathwater?

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.230
Some of these concerns are not misplaced.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.350
There's fear of labor market competition,

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.410
but there's also potential
benefits from hosting refugees,

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.760
including stimulating demand,
filling labor market gaps,

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:27.180
innovation, and exchange of knowledge.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:31.110
So we explore a specific
case of long-run encampment

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.800
in a place called Kakuma, Kenya,

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.800
which is in the northwestern
region of Kenya,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.480
on the border of South Sudan.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:44.100
And this camp has been
in existence since 1992.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.950
And we will explore...
Not only we'll look at

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.560
sort of the average of economic impacts,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.150
so that's also important to highlight.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:55.200
There's a lot of other impacts
that are relevant for policy.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.020
But as primarily an economist,

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.720
what we're studying here
are the economic impacts,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.940
and then we want to
explore relevant channels,

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.850
if we do find any impacts.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:09.090
The second question we asked was,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.100
well, how are refugees surviving?

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.330
So largely in most refugee
camps, countries are practicing

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.280
a policy of what they call enclosure.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.680
So refugees can't really leave the camp,

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.420
they can't work outside of the camps,

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:26.250
which means they have to live
off predominantly of food aid

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.020
or internal camp economy.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.820
So one of the things I'll
show you today is that there's

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.930
a flourishing economy
within the camp itself.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:37.920
And/or they live off of
remittances from relatives

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.100
who are either living in
the country that was fled

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:42.690
or living elsewhere in the world,

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.583
maybe in higher-income countries.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.470
And so what happened, this second question

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.340
sort of just landed in
our laps by happenstance.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.640
So when we were in the camp in 2015,

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.460
collecting the data
for the first question,

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.070
there was a sort of what we'd
call a natural experiment

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.890
that occurred where one
channel of remittances

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.970
was completely cut off and
we were able to measure

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.850
what happens when this
channel is shut down.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.030
So you can imagine that
obviously there would be impacts

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:18.330
on refugee households that
use this form of remittances,

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:21.870
but also if there are
inter-linkages economically

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:25.050
between the host and
the refugee community,

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:29.430
there could also be
externalities or flow-on effects

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:30.750
to the host community.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:33.123
And so we'll look at that also.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:38.520
So the context of our
study takes place in Kenya,

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.110
which obviously shares a border

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:45.110
with some highly-impacted
refugee sending regions

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.633
such as South Sudan,
Somalia, and Ethiopia.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:54.450
And we are focusing, I'm not
sure if you can see my cursor,

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.330
but in the very northwest corner of Kenya,

00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:01.350
on the border of South Sudan
is a region called Turkana.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.800
And we're looking specifically at a camp,

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.233
basically right about
there, called Kakuma.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:13.280
So something to know about
Turkana is that it's home

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.050
to the Turkana people.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.590
And the Turkana people are
a group of semi-nomadic

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:23.153
pastoralists who are largely disengaged

00:10:23.153 --> 00:10:26.610
with a formal economy, and
certainly in the early nineties

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.310
were disengaged with a formal economy.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:32.010
And they mostly herd cattle for a living.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.890
So they'll set up
semi-permanent residences

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:38.070
where they practice maybe a
little bit of horticulture.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:41.610
Mostly women are practicing
a little bit of horticulture,

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.160
and the men predominantly
are herding cattle.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:46.320
They'll go out for long periods of time

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.210
to graze their cattle,
and they'll come back

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:52.383
and then maybe they pick up
and move to another settlement.

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:59.250
Something to note about Turkana
is that it is extremely poor

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:00.240
and isolated.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.230
So this data comes from the 2005 census,

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.840
and that was the most recent data we had

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.190
at the time of publication.

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:13.190
I just checked the stats from
the 2018 census the other day,

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.030
and not much has changed.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:19.863
And so something to note
is that even when you...

00:11:19.863 --> 00:11:23.490
Kenya is a low and
middle-income country itself,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:26.970
but when you compare Turkana
to the rest of Kenya,

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:28.770
it's even poorer.

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:33.060
So 94%, I think now it's
around 90% of households

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.733
living in Turkana are
below the poverty line.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:40.350
Household sizes are larger than they are

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.930
even in other rural parts of Kenya.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.210
School attendance is extremely low.

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:48.843
That has not changed much
in the past almost 20 years.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:53.190
And households rely far less on farming

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.680
than the rest of Kenyan households.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.190
That's largely because the
area of Turkana is very arid.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:01.016
It's dry. I showed you the picture.

00:12:01.016 --> 00:12:06.016
It has a very scrubby set of
foliage. The soil is not great.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:12.480
And for one of those reasons,
for some of those reasons,

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.330
households largely rely on
cattle-herding for a living.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:18.720
So you can see the average
number of cattle, goats,

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.930
sheep owned by Turkana
households is much larger

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.483
than households in other parts of Kenya.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:32.810
So in the early nineties,
along and protracted conflict

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:41.330
in South Sudan intensified,
and 7,000 South Sudanese,

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:48.230
mostly young boys, walked
from South Sudan across

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:53.730
into Ethiopia temporarily and
over the border into Kenya,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:58.730
and these boys have been
referred to as the Lost Boys.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.550
And there's a lot that's
been written about them.

00:13:02.894 --> 00:13:05.940
If you haven't read the
book "What is the What"

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:10.860
by Dave Eggers, it's a
fantastic story, true story,

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:15.570
of one of these boys who fled South Sudan

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.850
and ultimately ended up
in the United States.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:22.850
But it gives a really intimate
portrayal of what these boys

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:24.753
endured in their journey.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:31.280
So in 1991, around 7,000
young boys walked into Kenya

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.780
and settled actually just over the border

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:38.670
in a town called Lokichogio.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:42.137
And by the following year, more
and more people had started

00:13:42.137 --> 00:13:47.137
to come over the border and
the government of Kenya agreed

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:50.340
to set up a refugee camp
in the town of Kakuma,

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:54.480
which at the time had a
population of less than 6,000.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:55.650
By the following year,

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:59.160
35,000 people were living in the camp,

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:03.810
and essentially overnight,
Kakuma became a city.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:06.750
And by 2015, at the time
when we were conducting

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.770
our field work, over
180,000 refugees were living

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:15.090
in Kakuma refugee camp, coming
mostly from South Sudan,

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.480
but from from other areas
as well, including Somalia,

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.393
the DRC, Ethiopia, and Darfur.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.650
And today Kakuma is one
of the longest-standing

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:30.000
refugee camps, and one of
the largest in the world.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.643
So here's an aerial shot of Kakuma.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:37.920
It is situated between two
rivers that are mostly dry,

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.520
but in the rainy seasons
they tend to flood,

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.123
and it consists of
semi-permanent structures.

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:52.730
But over time, an economy has developed.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.070
I mean, it's essentially a city now

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:57.900
and there's flourishing markets.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:01.440
So this is a picture of
one of the central markets

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.453
in the camp.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.890
This is Franco's Hotel,
which is a delicious

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:11.760
Ethiopian restaurant where we
would often meet with our team

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.740
for lunch and debriefings.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.520
But there's all kinds of markets
and businesses that operate

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:21.720
within the camp, restaurants,
little shops, tailors.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.240
It's really a flourishing economy.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.110
And there's a lot of exchange,
not only among the refugees,

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:30.780
but also among the host population.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.690
So in a lot... So Anne, our
colleague, is a sociologist,

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:35.730
she's a qualitative researcher.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:39.381
She did a lot of semi-structured
interviews with people.

00:15:39.381 --> 00:15:43.710
In a lot of these qualitative interviews,

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.290
it became clear that there's
just a very strong link

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:49.140
between the host and the refugee economy.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:53.280
So many of the Turkana
people use the refugee,

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.520
the markets in the camp,
as their main markets.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:00.450
There's also schools and
hospitals that are accessible

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:04.503
to both the refugee and the
host population within the camp.

00:16:07.779 --> 00:16:10.890
- [Emily] Sorry. Sarah, one question.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:15.890
Are the people in the refugee
camp able to go and work

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:19.740
outside or to work in general?

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:24.600
And if not, I mean,
what means do they use?

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.450
I mean, what currency do
they use at that point

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:30.280
in order to get the many, I don't know,

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:34.369
goods and services that have
developed inside this camp?

00:16:34.369 --> 00:16:37.500
- Yeah. So thank you for that question.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:40.350
First, just to answer quickly, no.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:45.350
So Kakuma practices,
Kenya practices a policy

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:48.840
of full encampment.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.660
So they are not, refugees
are not allowed to work

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:52.983
outside of the camp,

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:59.520
which means that they rely
predominantly on the food aid

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:02.550
that's given to them by UNHCR,

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:06.990
which consists mostly of
flour, oil, beans, and rice,

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:11.990
which they get, depending on
how large their household is,

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:16.680
they get once a month, a set amount.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:19.470
But something to note is actually that,

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:24.000
and I think this is specific to Kakuma,

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.010
I'm sure it's true in other camps,

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:29.520
but I do wanna highlight that the refugees

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.060
that are coming into the
camp, relatively speaking,

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:35.460
are wealthier than the Turkana people.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:39.420
So they're coming with more human capital,

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.820
they're also coming with more capital.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:45.810
And they have links to
people outside of the camp

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.220
that send them remittances.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.380
And so that's what
you'll see us talk about

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.450
in sort of the second part of the paper.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:55.680
But in the meantime, if they
can get enough capital to,

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.850
for instance, set up a
shop, a lot of refugees

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:04.850
have created their own businesses
and are living off of that

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:08.520
if they can, or remittances,

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:12.210
or in the worst of
circumstances just the food aid

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:13.440
that is given to them.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:17.070
They can also get employed
by UNHCR as translators

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:20.160
or as teachers or as medical workers,

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:23.867
to the extent that they have the training.

00:18:23.867 --> 00:18:27.030
And now, because the camp
has been there for so long,

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:31.650
there's a generation of people,
adults who have grown up,

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:35.254
who were either born in
the camp or moved there

00:18:35.254 --> 00:18:39.960
at a very young age and went
to school there and are now

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:43.713
essentially living in the camp
as if it's their hometown.

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:49.080
So when we think about
the possible ways in which

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:51.930
hosting refugees could
affect the local economy,

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:54.150
there are a couple of different...

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:57.930
The predictions are not clear upfront.

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:00.240
So the effects could operate

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:01.260
through many different channels.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:03.000
The first is a labor market channel.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.480
So one of the, I think the
primary concerns we hear

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.850
in higher-income countries
is, "What's gonna happen

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:11.490
when refugees come to our labor markets?

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:13.350
Are these going to depress wages?"

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:16.680
And as long as the locals are
employed in the same market

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:18.480
that refugees are trying to get jobs in,

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:20.730
this could depress wages.

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:24.270
On the other hand, if the
NGOs that are setting up

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:29.270
these refugee camps
demand labor and skills

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:31.410
that the local population has,

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.923
this could increase wages
through labor demand.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:39.420
Another way that refugee
camps could impact

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.610
the local economy is through competition

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:43.260
with aid-related goods.

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:47.657
So to the extent that UNHCR
and the WFP are pushing

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:53.540
a whole increased supply
of goods into the market,

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:57.720
if there are producers
locally who are also producing

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.430
these goods, that competition,

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:00.930
that's going to depress the price.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:03.750
First of all, the increase in
supply will depress the price.

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:05.610
If you're a producer,
that's gonna hurt you.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.220
But if you're a consumer,
if you're a net consumer,

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:09.990
that's gonna help you,
'cause now you're paying less

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.603
for things that you used to pay more for.

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:16.050
When we think about non-aid goods,

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:19.650
so things that UNHCR and
the WFP are not producing

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:22.020
but maybe are produced locally,

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:26.490
the increase in demand from
the shock in the population

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:27.960
will increase the price.

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:30.630
So that if you're a net
producer of these goods,

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.000
that will help you because
you can sell your goods

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.350
at a higher price.

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:35.490
But if you're a consumer,

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:37.680
if you're mostly just
consuming these goods,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.680
that increase in the price can hurt you.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:45.210
So it's not very clear
upfront how hosting refugees

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:46.830
is going to impact the economy.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:51.630
So we're gonna try and tease
out which of these channels

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:55.683
is happening and look
at the average effects.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.330
So to do this, we combine data

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:03.600
from many, many different data sources.

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:06.360
So the first thing we look at are

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:09.963
so that we can get a nice long-term view.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:13.770
We take data from nighttime satellites,

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.320
which have been pretty
established in the literature

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:19.590
as a good proxy for economic activity.

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:21.630
And even in a place like Turkana,

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:24.330
you can observe nighttime lights.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:27.000
Even though it is isolated and rural,

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.920
you do observe nighttime lights.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.617
At first we were like, "Maybe
we're not gonna see anything,"

00:21:30.617 --> 00:21:31.713
but you do.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:37.640
So we have the GPS data points
for 648 villages in Turkana,

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:43.140
which we then map to the
nighttime lights data in each year

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:46.170
from 1993 to 2013.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:50.070
And we combine that with data
on the refugee population

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:51.240
in each year.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.460
And so we're going to look essentially

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.600
at how far your village is from the camp,

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:01.290
and over time, as the
refugee population increases,

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:05.850
how does the economic activity
in your village fluctuate.

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:09.510
We combine that data also with some data

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:11.550
from the Hunger Safety Net Program,

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:14.310
which was an unconditional
cash transfer program

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:16.560
that was being rolled out
by the government of Kenya

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:20.040
at the time, and it's a
proxy-means tested program.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:24.540
So they surveyed over 140,000
households to get information

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:27.123
primarily on consumption
to see if they could,

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:29.850
if they were eligible for the program.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:33.090
And we have access to that
data so we can, at a snapshot,

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.530
at least cross-sectionally,
we can understand

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.230
consumption of these
households, again, at distances

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:43.110
that are near to the camp
and far from the camp.

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:47.700
We also combine that with
information on livestock prices

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:48.540
over time.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:51.240
So there's five key
livestock markets in Kenya,

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:53.580
one of which happens to
be located in Turkana.

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:57.390
And we can look at how prices
of livestock fluctuates

00:22:57.390 --> 00:23:02.390
in those markets over time as
refugees come in to the camp.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:05.820
And then finally we collect our own data

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.070
from June to July, 2015.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:11.370
We surveyed around 450 Turkana households,

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:12.930
so in the host population,

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:16.110
and around 170 refugee households.

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:20.220
So again, we have another
snapshot of cross-sectional data,

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:25.143
this time just from within the
camp and then around Turkana.

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:32.420
So we have two empirical
strategies that we will use

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:33.840
to answer this question.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:36.120
The first is to just look
at the nighttime lights

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.550
as a proxy for economic activity.

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:42.660
And we look at whether the
effect of the refugee population

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:46.770
increasing is stronger for
villages closer to the camp,

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.080
compared to far away from the camp.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:50.820
And then we'll corroborate this

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:54.390
with our cross-sectional
data, both from the HSNP,

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:59.390
the cash transfer survey,
again, looking at consumption

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:02.850
close to the camp versus
far away from the camp,

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:07.290
and then also with our own
survey data that we tried

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:10.350
to construct in the best
sort of counterfactual way

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.950
that we could, which is
we surveyed households

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:15.917
that were living in Kakuma town,

00:24:15.917 --> 00:24:20.610
so something that's important
to notice that host families,

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:22.710
they do not live in the camp,

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:27.210
they live outside of the camp
in Kakuma town and around.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:31.410
And then we chose two what
we call counterfactual towns

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:32.937
as our control towns.

00:24:32.937 --> 00:24:36.240
And the way that we
selected these towns is that

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:39.960
they had to have a similar
population to Kakuma in 1989,

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.540
so prior to the arrival of refugees.

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:46.380
And they were situated on
sort of the main artery

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:51.380
that connects Kitale, which is
a town in southwestern Kenya,

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:56.100
to Juba, which is the
capital of South Sudan.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:58.440
It's a main artery for
the transport of goods,

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:00.600
and obviously is gonna be
important economically.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.690
So we picked towns that were
about the same size as Kakuma

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:06.150
that were situated on this highway,

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:09.150
and then we stratified our random sampling

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:13.590
of these households by their distance

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:16.170
to the center of the town itself.

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:20.940
So here's a map of the Turkana
region, and here is Kakuma,

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.520
that's where the refugee camp is located.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:28.500
Our first counterfactual town
is a town called Lokichogio.

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:31.890
And actually Lokichogio
is also going to be,

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.140
we're actually gonna use
it not as a counterfactual

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:38.040
but as a placebo because
Lokichogio is where initially

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:41.850
all of the NGO operations
for the camp were running.

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:44.400
And so we would think
that that's obviously

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:45.843
an important place.

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:50.640
And we can also see if there's
something in particular

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:54.420
about just the NGOs being
there and creating an economy

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:55.740
just through the NGOs.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:59.160
Or if it's something specific
to actually having refugees,

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:01.143
which is what Kakuma has.

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:05.220
Our two counterfactual towns are Lorugum

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:08.097
which had a similar
population to Kakuma in 1989,

00:26:08.097 --> 00:26:12.420
and is situated on this main
artery leading to Uganda,

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:15.780
and Lokichar which again
meets the same criteria

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:17.850
and is situated on this highway.

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:20.750
And interestingly enough, right before,

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:23.280
in the couple of years
before we started collecting

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:26.070
the data, Lokichar had discovered oil

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:29.490
and was all of a sudden sort
of booming economically.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:33.390
So this is also an interesting
counterfactual place to study

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.723
because it obviously
has economic importance,

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.930
but it doesn't have refugees.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.757
So I won't bore you too much
with an estimating equation.

00:26:42.757 --> 00:26:45.030
Economists would obsess over this,

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:48.630
but just in general, what
we're doing is we're taking

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:50.130
as our dependent variable,

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:53.820
we have an index of nighttime lights.

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:55.860
We take the inverse hyperbolic sine,

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:57.374
which is essentially like a log function

00:26:57.374 --> 00:26:59.793
because there is skew in the data.

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:05.490
And we look at as our
independent variables,

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:10.490
we interact the population of
refugees living in the camp

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:13.020
in a particular year,

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:17.940
and we interact that with
how far away your village is.

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.010
So we're taking the nighttime lights

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:21.750
in a particular village.

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.830
So how far away is your
village from the camp itself.

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:27.870
And then we control for
lots of other things

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:30.630
that vary over time and across space.

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:33.240
So we control for the distance to a road,

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.553
which is obviously going
to be very important.

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:40.980
We control for village-fixed
effects and near-fixed effects,

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:45.090
which allow us to capture
unobservable factors

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:47.970
that don't change over time,

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:49.830
so that's the village-fixed effect,

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:53.940
and things that macroeconomic
factors that change over time,

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:56.400
with the year-fixed effects.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:01.140
And what I'll show you
next is a very messy table,

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.886
but I will walk you through it.

00:28:03.886 --> 00:28:08.310
In panel A we parametrize
our dependent variable

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:12.119
in a few different ways
just to really check

00:28:12.119 --> 00:28:14.760
how robust our estimates are.

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:18.510
And then we also look at
various different constraints

00:28:18.510 --> 00:28:19.343
on the data.

00:28:19.343 --> 00:28:23.670
So in panel A we're just using
what is the log, essentially,

00:28:23.670 --> 00:28:28.200
of the nighttime lights index
as the dependent variable.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:29.610
And in the first column we look

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.280
at our full sample of villages.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.710
In the second column,
we restrict the sample

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:38.550
to only villages that had a
similar population to Kakuma,

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.670
so 5,000 people or more in 1989.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:45.030
And in the third column,
we restrict our villages

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:47.430
to only villages that ever registered

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:51.480
any positive nighttime light
activity ever in history.

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:54.900
And what we can see is that
the further away from the camp

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:58.770
you get, and as the refugee
population increases,

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:01.110
the nighttime lights index goes down.

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:03.270
Or conversely, the closer you get,

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:06.660
the higher the nighttime lights indexes.

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.790
And that's what we're doing
in columns four through six,

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:10.920
is we just look at the inverse of that

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:13.115
as a measure of proximity.

00:29:13.115 --> 00:29:17.130
In panel B and panel C, we
do other things to account

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:19.620
for the fact that there are
lots of zeros in our data.

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:23.580
So in panel B we limit our
analysis of use a Tobit analysis,

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.530
which allows us to account for the fact

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:29.520
that we're truncated at zero,
we get very similar results.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:32.730
So the closer you are to the
camp, and the more refugees

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:36.060
there are, the higher the
nighttime lights indexes.

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:39.630
And then in panel C, we just
use a linear probability model,

00:29:39.630 --> 00:29:42.510
which is a zero one for do
you have any nighttime lights

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:43.830
index at all?

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:46.863
And again, very robust results.

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:51.120
What is I like to show
instead of that messy table

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:55.240
is this beautiful picture
which basically tells the story

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:58.620
in a picture.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:03.330
So first in panel A, what we're
looking at, is we take bins

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:07.380
of 10 kilometers away
from the camp, and we look

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:10.830
at the average of the
nighttime lights in that bin

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:13.860
as the refugee population increases.

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:17.580
And you can see really this
effect is coming from villages

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:20.073
that are within 10 kilometers of the camp.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:25.830
So a 10% increase in the
refugee population is associated

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:29.970
with 3.7% increase in the luminosity index

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:33.180
or a proxy of economic activity.

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:36.570
When we look at these
counterfactual and placebo towns,

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:40.380
so in panel B we say, "Okay,
let's just assume that the camp

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:44.160
was located in Lokichar, and
let's see what the effect

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:46.650
of the increasing refugee population is."

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:47.880
Well, there's no effect,

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:50.250
and that's what we would expect, right?

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:51.840
And then in panel C,

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:54.960
what we're looking at is
this placebo of Lokichogio,

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:58.590
which is the place where
originally all the NGO activity

00:30:58.590 --> 00:30:59.580
was occurring.

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:02.040
And what's fascinating
about this is, first of all,

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:04.740
you don't see any effect
close to Lokichogio,

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:07.770
but when we look at the
distance bin that's associated

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.170
with Kakuma, you see
the effect for Kakuma.

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:13.537
So this for us was very
reassuring, to say,

00:31:13.537 --> 00:31:15.180
"Look, there's something going on."

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:17.850
So as more refugees come into the camp,

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:22.110
economic activity is increasing over time.

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:24.870
What we then do is we take
our cross-sectional data

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:29.870
and we just look at
consumption using the HSNP data

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:33.600
at distance bins to Kakuma.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:37.170
And again, what we find is
that households that are living

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:39.870
within 10 kilometers of the camp,

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:44.340
their consumption is 25% higher
than these other households

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:47.400
that are living farther
away from the camp.

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:51.900
So these two estimates
allow us to essentially do

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:54.878
a back of the envelope
calculation and let us know

00:31:54.878 --> 00:31:57.690
what is the elasticity of consumption

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:00.360
with respect to refugee inflows.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:04.083
And that's about 0.55,
which is pretty high.

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:10.070
So with a 10% increase in
the refugee population.

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:15.840
We look at other mechanisms.

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:18.060
I won't go into the the estimation,

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:20.190
but we look at what are the mechanisms

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:22.320
that are potentially driving this result.

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:25.440
And it appears that there
are two main mechanisms.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.290
The first is that is
through the labor market.

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:32.790
So for households that
live near to the camp,

00:32:32.790 --> 00:32:36.240
there's more employment in
low-skilled occupations.

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:38.850
So remember at the time
of the establishment

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.490
of the refugee camp, there
essentially was no labor market

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:42.480
for Turkana people.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:45.543
They were just herding
cattle, essentially.

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:51.480
Now, presently, there
are people who are living

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:56.280
near the camp, working for
UNHCR in low-skilled occupations

00:32:56.280 --> 00:33:01.280
as cleaners, as cooks, as
drivers, as greeting officers

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.343
when people come into the camp.

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:10.380
The other main channel that we find

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:12.807
is through the livestock market.

00:33:12.807 --> 00:33:15.570
And this is pretty intuitive
when you think about it.

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:20.010
So refugees get a food
aid package from the WFP,

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.060
the World Food Program,

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:23.220
and that consists of flour, oil, beans,

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:26.430
and rice essentially, but there's no meat.

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:29.013
And most of these cultures eat meat.

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:33.300
And what do the Turkana
have? They have meat.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:38.300
So there's an exchange
between Turkana producers

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:42.450
who can sell their meat in
the main livestock market

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:46.950
in Kakuma and refugee
households who are able

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:47.998
to buy this meat.

00:33:47.998 --> 00:33:52.440
And so what we find is
that the livestock prices

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:57.440
closer to the camp are rising
with the influx of refugees.

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:01.770
And so the takeaways from this first paper

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:04.290
really is that there's
considerable economic links

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:08.130
between the refugee and the
host community in this context.

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:10.710
And at least in this context,

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:13.620
which is one of the
largest camps in the world,

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:16.620
refugee camps do generate
some positive benefits

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:17.617
for the local economy.

00:34:17.617 --> 00:34:20.010
And there's obviously caveats.

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:23.190
We look at a particular
camp in a rural area,

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:25.650
this is not representative of all camps,

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:29.130
but I would say
representative of many camps.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:30.990
But obviously the effects
are gonna be different

00:34:30.990 --> 00:34:31.823
in other settings.

00:34:31.823 --> 00:34:35.490
I can't say anything about
what are these effects

00:34:35.490 --> 00:34:38.253
going to be in the US or
in Europe or in Australia.

00:34:39.180 --> 00:34:41.730
Furthermore, our
counterfactual is not perfect.

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:45.120
We did the best that we could
with the data that we had,

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:46.140
but it's not perfect.

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:48.660
So I'm not necessarily
claiming that it's causal,

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:50.823
but it's highly, highly suggestive.

00:34:52.140 --> 00:34:54.090
And what we are observing
here are average effects.

00:34:54.090 --> 00:34:55.830
So we don't look at any heterogeneity,

00:34:55.830 --> 00:34:58.623
we can't do a cost
benefit analysis and say,

00:34:59.767 --> 00:35:04.767
"The benefit of hosting refugees
will be this much in GDP."

00:35:04.917 --> 00:35:06.480
We can't say that.

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:09.270
But what we can say is
that there's a strong link

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:12.810
between the host and the refugee community

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:15.243
that benefits both.

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:21.690
So what we do in the
second paper is we exploit

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:23.910
this natural experiment that happened

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:25.950
right as we were just getting ready

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:28.950
to collect the data for the first paper.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:31.080
There was, I don't know if
any of you remember this,

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:32.760
but there was a terrorist attack

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:34.560
on Garissa University College,

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:39.330
which is in the northeast
of Kenya, in April, 2015.

00:35:39.330 --> 00:35:42.870
An Al Shabaab claimed
responsibility for that attack

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:46.770
where over a hundred people died

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:49.290
and many people were injured.

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:52.290
The Kenyan government
was obviously distressed,

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:56.940
and as a result expressed
concern about the lack

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.610
of oversight of money-sending agencies,

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:02.440
specifically what they were
calling Muslim transfers

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:06.480
through a transfer system known as hawala.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:08.880
So I'll get into the details
of hawala in a minute,

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:11.490
but it's essentially a
money exchange rooted

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:16.020
in Islamic finance, and
mostly limited people

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:19.980
who are using hawala are mostly Muslim.

00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:21.990
So the Kenyan government
says, "All right, nope,

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:23.190
we're shutting down hawala

00:36:23.190 --> 00:36:25.830
because this is funding
terrorist activities.

00:36:25.830 --> 00:36:30.330
And so the central bank shut
down all hawala transfers,

00:36:30.330 --> 00:36:33.300
starting on April 8th, 2015,

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:38.280
and reopened them again in June of 2015.

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:40.530
So we were already collecting our data,

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:43.170
we started to collect
our data in early June.

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:47.617
So we're able to... We asked
households, retrospectively,

00:36:47.617 --> 00:36:49.680
"What was your consumption before April?"

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:52.020
And then, "What is your consumption now?"

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:56.730
And we're able to try and
understand how this shock

00:36:56.730 --> 00:36:58.770
to remittances may have affected

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:03.153
not only refugee households,
but Turkana households as well.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:05.940
So how does hawala work?

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:09.360
Hawala is an informal
way of transferring money

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:10.860
between two people.

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:13.567
So essentially, you're
person A and you say,

00:37:13.567 --> 00:37:16.473
"I wanna send money to person B,

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:22.170
and I'm gonna do that by
going to my hawala dealer,

00:37:22.170 --> 00:37:23.160
person X."

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:26.130
And you say, "Okay, I wanna
send my friend some money."

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:28.650
And the dealer says, "Okay, great.

00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:30.120
How much do you wanna send them?

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:33.840
And what is the password
that they need to give

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:34.697
to pick up the money?"

00:37:34.697 --> 00:37:37.230
So you give them that information.

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:39.720
The hawala dealer then
goes to their friend,

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.747
hawala dealer M, who's nearby
to your friend and says,

00:37:43.747 --> 00:37:47.097
"Okay, they're sending the
money. Here's the money."

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:50.580
They don't actually exchange.

00:37:50.580 --> 00:37:54.240
So the dealers don't
necessarily exchange money

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:55.380
directly right away.

00:37:55.380 --> 00:37:57.690
This is an informal transfer system

00:37:57.690 --> 00:37:59.190
based on the honor system.

00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:01.950
So at some point, these people,

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:03.843
these dealers will settle up,

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:07.890
but there isn't necessarily
an actual exchange of money

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:08.723
at the time.

00:38:08.723 --> 00:38:11.040
So it's really based on
the honor system and trust,

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:14.850
and there's no interest, there's no fees.

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:16.800
The fees are very low because it follows

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:18.930
Islamic financial principles.

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:20.550
So then the dealer
says, "All right, great.

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:21.870
I have the information."

00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:23.610
Your friend comes in to pick up the money,

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:25.950
they give the password,
they get the money.

00:38:25.950 --> 00:38:28.143
And that's how hawala works.

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:34.020
In spite of its informalness,
there are formal businesses.

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:36.240
So this is a picture of Dahabshiil,

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:41.240
which is the largest hawala
dealer in Kenya, certainly.

00:38:42.270 --> 00:38:46.380
So it is formalized institutionally,

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:48.540
but it's really based on the honor system,

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:50.940
and the fees are really low.

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:52.597
So the government of Kenya says,

00:38:52.597 --> 00:38:56.850
"All right, no hawala transfers."

00:38:56.850 --> 00:38:59.370
However, things like Western Union

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:04.370
and other forms of mobile money transfers

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:08.190
were still able to
function over this time.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:11.628
So when we think about how this shutdown

00:39:11.628 --> 00:39:15.030
could impact first refugees.

00:39:15.030 --> 00:39:17.670
Obviously there would be
direct negative effects

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:21.003
on households who are
previously using remittances,

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:26.010
but there could be positive externalities

00:39:26.010 --> 00:39:29.580
or positive spillover
effects on other households

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:32.603
if the decrease in purchasing
from the households

00:39:32.603 --> 00:39:35.370
who are affected lower prices.

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:38.670
So if you have a decrease in
demand from the households

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:40.800
who can no longer buy
what they were buying,

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:42.330
this will push prices down.

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:45.386
And if you weren't affected,
this is positive for you

00:39:45.386 --> 00:39:47.973
because you can buy things for cheaper.

00:39:49.320 --> 00:39:52.890
But if you're a household
that is selling those goods,

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:55.350
you will be hurt by those lower prices.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:57.840
So again, the effects
are kind of ambiguous

00:39:57.840 --> 00:39:59.642
or at least heterogeneous.

00:39:59.642 --> 00:40:01.590
And so we'll try to exploit,

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:05.340
we'll try to look at this
heterogeneity in our analysis.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:09.690
When you think about Turkana
households or host households,

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:11.910
there will be just like producers,

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:15.480
refugee producers or refugee
sellers, good sellers,

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:17.160
there would be negative
effects on households

00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:21.180
who are selling items to
refugees, if the demand

00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:24.224
for those items falls as a result

00:40:24.224 --> 00:40:26.583
of their reduction in income.

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:32.100
But again, if you're a household
who's consuming these goods

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:33.930
that have the price lowered,

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:37.620
then you benefit from having
a lower price of consumption.

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:39.870
Or there could just be
no effect on households

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:42.371
if you're uninvolved in markets at all.

00:40:42.371 --> 00:40:45.630
And some Turkana households
are not involved in markets,

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:47.640
although, as we established
in the first paper,

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:50.970
the ones that live around
the camp certainly are

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:52.413
involved in markets.

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:58.350
So to look at this, we
interviewed hawaladeers.

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:02.250
So there are hawala
dealers inside of the camp

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:04.350
that we were able to interview

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:07.590
and get some qualitative information from.

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:09.395
And then we also have
the two household surveys

00:41:09.395 --> 00:41:13.080
that we were conducting right
at the time of the shutdown.

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:15.660
So we have the refugee household survey,

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:19.650
which we conducted from
June 9th to the 20th,

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:21.810
so that the ban was lifted on the 22nd.

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:24.570
So all of the refugees in
our household we interviewed

00:41:24.570 --> 00:41:29.570
while they were currently
unable to access hawala.

00:41:29.730 --> 00:41:32.520
The Turkana household,
roughly the same time,

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:35.280
we have a few households
that we interviewed

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:40.099
after the ban was lifted.

00:41:40.099 --> 00:41:43.320
But we asked a lot of different
information in the household

00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:46.080
on their demographics,
on their employment.

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:47.520
We had a consumption schedule.

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:50.130
So that's largely what
we'll use to understand

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:51.120
what are the impacts.

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:54.900
And we asked households, "What
was your consumption like

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:56.490
in the first week of April

00:41:56.490 --> 00:41:58.500
versus at the time of the survey?"

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:02.910
So obviously a caveat is
that the pre-data we have

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:07.773
is retrospective, but
nonetheless, we do our best.

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:12.753
So when we interviewed
these hawala dealers,

00:42:12.753 --> 00:42:15.634
they tell us that the remittance
shutdown was complete.

00:42:15.634 --> 00:42:20.223
It wasn't forced. They could
not make any transfers.

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:25.440
When we talked to them about
what their transfers were like

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:28.500
prior to the shutdown, they were enormous.

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:33.500
So the average monthly
transfers for Dahabshiil

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:40.850
was about $60,000 USD.

00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:45.232
When we look across these
five dealers in the camp,

00:42:45.232 --> 00:42:50.232
what they report suggests
that there's 2.4 million USD

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:52.800
in transfers occurring annually.

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:54.630
This is enormous.

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:59.630
And there are other researchers
who prior to the shutdown,

00:43:00.365 --> 00:43:03.900
have been doing work
on hawala in the camp,

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:05.670
and this data is completely consistent

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:07.890
with what they have collected as well.

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:11.700
So, I mean, this is an enormous
amount of economic activity

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:13.380
that's occurring.

00:43:13.380 --> 00:43:17.073
And that was stopped
for a couple of months.

00:43:18.600 --> 00:43:21.830
When we interviewed refugees,
what we found is that 60%

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:25.830
of refugees had received
remittances in the past year.

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:29.970
The average size of the last
transfer was about $200,

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:32.940
and they receive around
six transfers per year.

00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:36.150
So again, this is a lot
of economic activity

00:43:36.150 --> 00:43:39.630
coming into the camp.

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:44.040
When you compare that to
the GDP per capita in Kenya,

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:49.040
which was around $1,500 USD
in 2016, this is significant.

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:54.600
54% of remittances occur through hawala.

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:56.580
This is largely due to the
fact that there's a huge

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:01.230
Somali population in
the camp who are Muslim,

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:04.620
and there are other smaller
populations as well.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:06.960
But this is also, as a researcher,

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:10.170
this was nice for us,
because we have variations.

00:44:10.170 --> 00:44:12.390
So not everyone is receiving hawala,

00:44:12.390 --> 00:44:17.080
some people are receiving
hawala and we can exploit

00:44:18.206 --> 00:44:21.450
that heterogeneity to
really try and understand

00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:22.713
the various channels.

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:28.740
45% of the remittance
receivers who we interviewed,

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.860
they reported that they
experienced a decrease

00:44:31.860 --> 00:44:34.353
in remittances since the shutdown.

00:44:36.930 --> 00:44:39.780
So when we just look before and after,

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:43.260
we just compare everyone
in our refugee sample.

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:47.100
So 170 households, we compare
their consumption in April

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:50.250
to their consumption in
June with a simple T test,

00:44:50.250 --> 00:44:51.960
we don't find any differences.

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:56.280
So there was no change on
average, but as I mentioned,

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:59.247
there's significant
heterogeneity in this population.

00:44:59.247 --> 00:45:03.390
And so if the first level
of heterogeneity we look at

00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:06.150
is just, "Do you receive transfers

00:45:06.150 --> 00:45:08.755
or do you not receive transfers?"

00:45:08.755 --> 00:45:12.573
And you can see... Regardless
of whether it's hawala.

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:18.770
So you can see that
people whose languages...

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:21.630
We didn't ask people,
"What is your religion?"

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:26.010
But we know the language
that the interview

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:30.420
was conducted in, and people
from Somalia, Nuba, and Darfur

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:32.337
are mostly Muslim.

00:45:32.337 --> 00:45:35.820
And so we use that as our
proxy sort of for how exposed

00:45:35.820 --> 00:45:38.547
to hawala would you be.

00:45:38.547 --> 00:45:43.547
And so people who we think
would be largely most likely

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:48.840
to use hawala and who had
received transfers previously,

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:49.673
there's a big difference

00:45:49.673 --> 00:45:53.403
in their consumption before and after.

00:45:54.480 --> 00:45:58.170
And people who've been in the camp longer

00:45:58.170 --> 00:46:02.990
were more affected, had
a lower consumption.

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:08.463
I'm sorry, their transfers were affected.

00:46:09.870 --> 00:46:13.080
But they actually, the people...

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:14.730
Yeah, their transfers
were affected, sorry.

00:46:14.730 --> 00:46:18.990
And people who have household
enterprises were affected.

00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:21.240
So these are the business
owners who are probably using

00:46:21.240 --> 00:46:25.230
remittances to fund their businesses.

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:29.310
And again, people whose
income was largely...

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:31.650
Larger share of their income
was coming from businesses,

00:46:31.650 --> 00:46:36.510
as opposed to being employed
or to selling animals,

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:40.893
they also were affected by the shutdown.

00:46:42.240 --> 00:46:45.360
Again, these are just
simple comparisons of needs.

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:50.360
So then we look again at
those who used hawala,

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:54.660
specifically, and it's a
similar pattern, right?

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:59.613
So people who are more likely
to use hawala were impacted.

00:47:00.690 --> 00:47:03.607
People who said their transfers...

00:47:03.607 --> 00:47:06.450
People who use hawala
were more likely to say

00:47:06.450 --> 00:47:10.923
that their transfers had
decreased since April,

00:47:11.850 --> 00:47:15.270
they are less likely
to be selling animals.

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:17.190
So this is consistent
with what we would think

00:47:17.190 --> 00:47:20.640
of the characteristics of
people who would be impacted

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:22.083
by the shutdown.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:28.650
Then we try to estimate
the effect of this shutdown

00:47:28.650 --> 00:47:32.610
on consumption for users compared

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:35.220
to non-users before and after the shock.

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:38.520
So this is empirically something
similar to what we'd call

00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:40.230
a difference in differences.

00:47:40.230 --> 00:47:44.040
The only caveat is that we
don't have a whole panel

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:45.540
of pre-data.

00:47:45.540 --> 00:47:47.850
We're really just looking
at these two periods,

00:47:47.850 --> 00:47:51.150
one of which was a retrospective survey,

00:47:51.150 --> 00:47:53.490
but nonetheless could be informative.

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:58.490
So what we look at is your
consumption in April and in June,

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:03.738
and we regress that on a dummy for whether

00:48:03.738 --> 00:48:06.960
this was the June period
that you reported.

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:11.100
And then we interact that
with sources of heterogeneity.

00:48:11.100 --> 00:48:14.400
So did you use hawala in the past?

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:16.140
Were you interviewed in a language

00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:19.473
that is associated with Islam?

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:23.130
Did you or do you earn wages primarily?

00:48:23.130 --> 00:48:24.813
Do you own a business?

00:48:25.830 --> 00:48:30.830
And then we, sorry, we include
a household-fixed effect

00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:34.860
to control for unobservable
factors about the household

00:48:34.860 --> 00:48:36.663
that don't change over time.

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:39.510
And what do we find?

00:48:39.510 --> 00:48:43.560
So the first thing we find
is that for households

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:45.900
who received hawala in the past,

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:48.480
when we look at their consumption in June

00:48:48.480 --> 00:48:51.570
compared to their consumption in April,

00:48:51.570 --> 00:48:56.570
what we find is there's a
decrease in some pretty key goods,

00:48:57.330 --> 00:49:01.020
which include milk and meat,
which also happen to come

00:49:01.020 --> 00:49:04.350
from things like cows and goats and sheep

00:49:04.350 --> 00:49:07.093
that they're not producing,

00:49:07.093 --> 00:49:09.390
the Turkana households are producing.

00:49:09.390 --> 00:49:11.884
So already we sort of
anticipate this might have

00:49:11.884 --> 00:49:15.063
a spillover effect onto
the local community.

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:19.830
And this decrease in
consumption is pretty large.

00:49:19.830 --> 00:49:24.300
So it's a 26 to 33%
decrease in the consumption

00:49:24.300 --> 00:49:28.293
of these goods per
households that use hawala.

00:49:29.629 --> 00:49:34.629
When we look at households
who don't receive hawala,

00:49:35.729 --> 00:49:39.200
that's what this coefficient captures is,

00:49:39.200 --> 00:49:42.510
is that there's actually an
increase in the consumption

00:49:42.510 --> 00:49:44.730
of these, and these would
be considered luxury goods.

00:49:44.730 --> 00:49:46.530
So things like sugar, milk, and meat,

00:49:46.530 --> 00:49:48.330
they don't come in the aid package.

00:49:48.330 --> 00:49:50.940
They're not things that
people eat every day normally,

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:52.560
They are more expensive.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:55.560
And so when you look at households
who didn't receive hawala

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:57.600
and you look at their consumption in June

00:49:57.600 --> 00:49:59.820
compared to their consumption in April,

00:49:59.820 --> 00:50:02.370
we see an increase in the
consumption of these luxury goods.

00:50:02.370 --> 00:50:04.680
So this suggests there
must have been some kind

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:07.710
of price impact on these goods,

00:50:07.710 --> 00:50:09.840
if you can increase your
consumption of them,

00:50:09.840 --> 00:50:11.160
that they're probably cheaper.

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:12.840
And that would be driven by the fact

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:16.440
that the hawala households
have reduced their demand

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:17.403
for these goods.

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:22.620
We run the same estimation just looking

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:25.110
at a different measure of hawala exposure.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:27.660
So instead of the direct
question, "Do you use hawala?"

00:50:27.660 --> 00:50:29.040
we just look at what language

00:50:29.040 --> 00:50:31.140
was the interview conducted in,

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:33.300
and we find a very similar effect here.

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:37.388
So households that speak
Somali, Nuba, or Darfuri

00:50:37.388 --> 00:50:41.340
have reduced their consumption
of milk and meat in June

00:50:41.340 --> 00:50:43.290
compared to April.

00:50:43.290 --> 00:50:46.770
And we do still see, at
least a positive increase

00:50:46.770 --> 00:50:49.983
for sugar in these other households.

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:56.400
Then we look at more heterogeneous impacts

00:50:56.400 --> 00:50:57.810
by the income source.

00:50:57.810 --> 00:51:01.050
So were you a wage worker?

00:51:01.050 --> 00:51:03.210
Did you own your own business?

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:08.210
And again, we still use
the interaction of hawala.

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:13.620
So again, we still see this
reduction in consumption

00:51:13.620 --> 00:51:18.620
for hawala households, but
we don't really see an impact

00:51:19.440 --> 00:51:24.440
on wage workers or people
who own businesses, so...

00:51:24.487 --> 00:51:29.487
Then we look at the Turkana households.

00:51:30.420 --> 00:51:33.000
So we have reason to
believe there could be,

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:35.280
especially with the
reduction of meat and milk,

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:37.350
there could be spillover effects

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:38.610
onto the Turkana households,

00:51:38.610 --> 00:51:40.440
particularly if they're
producing these things,

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:43.710
if they're selling these
goods to the refugees.

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:47.160
And so we leverage, again,
our empirical strategy.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:51.270
We're looking at households
that are close to the camp

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:54.900
and far away from the camp
using those counterfactual towns

00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:56.970
I had told you about before.

00:51:56.970 --> 00:52:00.180
And we're looking at their
change in consumption

00:52:00.180 --> 00:52:02.373
between April and June,

00:52:04.500 --> 00:52:07.800
depending on whether they
live in the Kakuma sample

00:52:07.800 --> 00:52:11.580
or if they are farther
outside of the Kakuma sample.

00:52:11.580 --> 00:52:13.470
And then we also look
at their heterogeneity

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:14.640
by their income source.

00:52:14.640 --> 00:52:18.330
Are they producing livestock
or are they doing other things?

00:52:18.330 --> 00:52:20.913
Are they more exposed
to this shock or not?

00:52:23.070 --> 00:52:28.070
And what we find is
that, in terms of things

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:34.620
that we think would
confound this analysis,

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:38.520
their income and assets between households

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:41.130
who live in Kakuma versus
don't live in Kakuma

00:52:41.130 --> 00:52:43.470
are basically the same.

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:47.010
So in terms of wealth, on average,

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:48.510
these households are the same.

00:52:48.510 --> 00:52:50.880
So we would be concerned,
right, if for example,

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:55.680
households living near Kakuma
were just poorer on average.

00:52:55.680 --> 00:52:59.100
That would suggest that
if we did find any effect,

00:52:59.100 --> 00:53:01.110
it's just because these
households are poor,

00:53:01.110 --> 00:53:02.910
we don't have reason to
believe that would be poorer.

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:04.980
In fact, we think they would be richer

00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:07.290
because they're so
linked into this economy.

00:53:07.290 --> 00:53:12.000
But on average we find that
they're basically the same.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:16.230
But what we do find is if
we look at their consumption

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:19.350
in June, it's much
lower for the households

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:22.233
that are living near Kakuma.

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:26.670
And we do find that they've
reported they've decreased

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:29.400
their food consumption.

00:53:29.400 --> 00:53:32.830
They're more likely to have
decreased their food consumption

00:53:33.720 --> 00:53:37.170
over time between April and June.

00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:41.460
So then we estimate more
formally this impact.

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:45.367
And what we just look at
a simple regression is,

00:53:45.367 --> 00:53:48.660
"What was the change in your
purchase of sugar and tea

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:51.990
and milk, these more luxury goods?"

00:53:51.990 --> 00:53:53.850
If you're in the Kakuma sample,

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:58.380
your consumption decreased
for sugar and tea.

00:53:58.380 --> 00:54:02.250
This is just if you purchased
it on the intensive margin,

00:54:02.250 --> 00:54:04.080
it doesn't seem like there's any effect.

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:06.330
But whether external margin,

00:54:06.330 --> 00:54:08.040
did you purchase or did you not purchase?

00:54:08.040 --> 00:54:11.125
You're much less likely that
to purchase these things

00:54:11.125 --> 00:54:13.590
if you're living in Kakuma.

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:16.470
And then we look at the
heterogeneity by how exposed

00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:17.610
to this shock would you be.

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:18.840
Well, if you're selling animals,

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:20.670
you're gonna be more exposed to this shock

00:54:20.670 --> 00:54:23.880
because we already
demonstrated that the demand

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:25.170
for these goods decreased.

00:54:25.170 --> 00:54:27.390
And in fact, we find that
this is really driven

00:54:27.390 --> 00:54:31.140
by households who are selling animals.

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:33.870
And the probability of
consumption has decreased

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:38.583
by 12 and 15% for sugar
and tea, respectively.

00:54:41.010 --> 00:54:44.010
So what we find here is that, on average,

00:54:44.010 --> 00:54:47.460
refugees are not hurt by the shutdown.

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:50.973
But the more exposed
you were to this shock,

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:54.540
not surprisingly, the bigger the effect.

00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:56.550
So hawala-receiving refugees,

00:54:56.550 --> 00:54:58.990
they reduced their
consumption of key goods

00:55:01.020 --> 00:55:06.020
as a result of this shock,
from 23 to 29%, including meat,

00:55:06.210 --> 00:55:09.420
which they buy from
the Turkana households.

00:55:09.420 --> 00:55:13.440
Other groups were not as
affected by this shock.

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:15.990
And if anything, non-hawala households

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:18.730
were positively affected by this shock

00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:24.423
through the reduction
in price of these goods.

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:28.860
We find spillover effects
to Turkana households,

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:32.667
particularly those who are
producing meat and milk

00:55:32.667 --> 00:55:35.580
and these goods that hawala households

00:55:35.580 --> 00:55:37.350
reduce their consumption of.

00:55:37.350 --> 00:55:41.160
And so that results in a
reduction of the purchase

00:55:41.160 --> 00:55:44.280
of key luxury goods, sugar and tea,

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:46.410
by the Turkana households.

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:50.400
And so I think the main
takeaway from this paper

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:53.010
is that things like blunt policies,

00:55:53.010 --> 00:55:55.560
like blockages to remittances,

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:58.710
they can have a really negative effect,

00:55:58.710 --> 00:56:02.220
especially on the populations
they weren't intended

00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:03.053
to impact.

00:56:03.053 --> 00:56:08.030
And so we need to be thoughtful
about how we approach

00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:10.713
these kinds of policies.

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:15.270
So that's all I have for you today.

00:56:15.270 --> 00:56:17.160
I'm a little bit early,
but I hope that we can have

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:18.540
a nice discussion.

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:22.390
This is just a picture that
I took of a Turkana household

00:56:23.250 --> 00:56:26.256
outside of Kakuma, and
it's pretty representative

00:56:26.256 --> 00:56:27.933
of their way of life.

00:56:29.430 --> 00:56:31.980
And yeah, so I hope that
you learned something,

00:56:31.980 --> 00:56:34.023
and I look forward to our discussion.

00:56:36.090 --> 00:56:38.820
- Great. Thank you so much, Sarah,

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:42.030
for this really engaging presentation.

00:56:42.030 --> 00:56:43.980
So for those of you in the audience,

00:56:43.980 --> 00:56:46.110
please feel free to use
the raised hand feature

00:56:46.110 --> 00:56:49.440
if you have any specific
questions, or use the chat.

00:56:49.440 --> 00:56:53.370
I'll exercise my privilege
of going first to ask.

00:56:53.370 --> 00:56:56.700
So especially given that this is kind of

00:56:56.700 --> 00:56:59.880
a more established project
and one that you're sort of,

00:56:59.880 --> 00:57:01.680
have kind of closed the book on,

00:57:01.680 --> 00:57:04.980
I wanted to ask you, in particular
for some of the students

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:07.920
in the audience, to reflect a
little bit on the experience

00:57:07.920 --> 00:57:09.210
of doing this research project.

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:10.871
So a few things that
are obviously striking,

00:57:10.871 --> 00:57:14.040
this is a very challenging
area to do research in

00:57:14.040 --> 00:57:15.870
for a number of reasons.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:17.610
So if you could talk a
little about your field work

00:57:17.610 --> 00:57:19.110
and some of that.

00:57:19.110 --> 00:57:21.030
One thing that's really
striking and really lovely,

00:57:21.030 --> 00:57:24.423
I think, about the research
and the presentation is,

00:57:25.530 --> 00:57:28.530
you're very forthcoming about
some of the shortcomings,

00:57:28.530 --> 00:57:30.270
but you do a lot of
creative things, I think,

00:57:30.270 --> 00:57:33.330
to get some empirical leverage
in places that I think

00:57:33.330 --> 00:57:34.650
would be really hard to get like that

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:36.180
perfect identification strategy.

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:38.280
So I'd also love to hear a little bit

00:57:38.280 --> 00:57:39.420
about your thought process,

00:57:39.420 --> 00:57:41.490
how you kind of developed those things.

00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:43.500
- Yeah, thanks. Thanks for that, Emily.

00:57:43.500 --> 00:57:45.720
Yeah, first I'll speak
about the fieldwork.

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:49.380
So I mean, I've done a lot
of fieldwork in my life,

00:57:49.380 --> 00:57:51.570
and this certainly was
not the first fieldwork

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:52.800
that I had ever done.

00:57:52.800 --> 00:57:56.220
But to this day, remains the
most challenging fieldwork

00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:57.720
I've ever done.

00:57:57.720 --> 00:58:00.360
I mean, first of all, just the condition,

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:02.760
the environmental
conditions are very tough.

00:58:02.760 --> 00:58:07.760
So we were there in May,
June, July, and August,

00:58:08.820 --> 00:58:10.470
which are some of the hottest months.

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:13.380
It's very arid, it's very dusty.

00:58:13.380 --> 00:58:15.540
It was just physically very...

00:58:15.540 --> 00:58:17.220
And I wasn't even conducting interviews,

00:58:17.220 --> 00:58:20.280
I was just managing a
field team and going out

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:23.460
for a couple hours to check
on people and then debriefing

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:25.623
at the lovely Ethiopian restaurant.

00:58:26.760 --> 00:58:30.840
And that in itself was
the most challenging work

00:58:30.840 --> 00:58:34.480
I've ever done, environmentally, but also

00:58:36.231 --> 00:58:41.231
it's a difficult region
politically in terms of security.

00:58:42.930 --> 00:58:46.889
So at the time we had to
travel with armed guards

00:58:46.889 --> 00:58:48.300
everywhere we went.

00:58:48.300 --> 00:58:51.780
So, I guess I should take an
even bigger step back and say,

00:58:51.780 --> 00:58:55.680
this work would not have
been possible if it were not

00:58:55.680 --> 00:58:58.500
for the support of the
World Bank and UNHCR.

00:58:58.500 --> 00:59:00.330
So essentially the way we came into...

00:59:00.330 --> 00:59:03.457
We didn't just rock up
to the camp and say,

00:59:03.457 --> 00:59:05.207
"Hey, we wanna do some interviews."

00:59:06.480 --> 00:59:10.920
This kind of work requires a
lot of institutional support.

00:59:10.920 --> 00:59:15.570
So we were invited by the
World Bank and the UNHCR

00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:17.220
to conduct this study.

00:59:17.220 --> 00:59:22.220
And the first paper
resulted in a policy paper

00:59:22.710 --> 00:59:26.730
that the World Bank has
published and it informed policy

00:59:26.730 --> 00:59:30.060
in Kenya about what they were
going to do with the camps.

00:59:30.060 --> 00:59:32.820
So to do this kind of fieldwork,

00:59:32.820 --> 00:59:34.260
and speaking to the students in the room,

00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:36.540
to do this kind of fieldwork requires

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:41.100
a very strong institutional
partner and support,

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:43.380
because otherwise it's not possible,

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:45.810
but it's also not appropriate, right?

00:59:45.810 --> 00:59:50.810
So I think there was a lot
of conversation between UNHCR

00:59:51.750 --> 00:59:56.750
and the World Bank and
local Turkana leaders.

00:59:57.120 --> 01:00:00.120
So the very first thing
that we did when we arrived

01:00:00.120 --> 01:00:02.940
in Turkana, even though we
had the support of UNHCR

01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:05.400
and the World Bank, we
went to each of these

01:00:05.400 --> 01:00:08.400
counterfactual towns and we
met with the political leaders

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:11.407
of those towns, introduced
ourselves and said,

01:00:11.407 --> 01:00:14.940
"Here's what we're doing.
Here's who we're working with.

01:00:14.940 --> 01:00:18.690
Do we have your support to
work in your community?"

01:00:18.690 --> 01:00:23.690
And those were long conversations,
and is the first step

01:00:26.910 --> 01:00:30.897
you have to do when you conduct
this kind of field work.

01:00:30.897 --> 01:00:34.260
And thankfully we got the
support, but yeah, I mean,

01:00:34.260 --> 01:00:35.820
it was intense, it was intense.

01:00:35.820 --> 01:00:40.410
And anytime we traveled
to these other towns,

01:00:40.410 --> 01:00:43.470
we really were accompanied by armed guards,

01:00:43.470 --> 01:00:48.470
and I had never experiencing
anything like that prior,

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:50.190
and I haven't since.

01:00:50.190 --> 01:00:54.153
So yeah, it was hard.

01:00:57.120 --> 01:01:01.121
And then in terms of
the empirical strategy,

01:01:01.121 --> 01:01:05.970
I have one foot in the
development economics world,

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:10.020
and I think, as many of you
know, that's the world of RCTs. 
(Randomized Control Trials)

01:01:10.020 --> 01:01:14.430
And I've certainly done
RCTs and they're appropriate

01:01:14.430 --> 01:01:15.750
for certain questions,

01:01:15.750 --> 01:01:18.690
but this is very clearly something
that you can't randomize,

01:01:18.690 --> 01:01:23.690
and not everything and/or
should be answered with an RCT.

01:01:23.983 --> 01:01:28.057
And so I think what we
said to ourselves is like,

01:01:28.057 --> 01:01:32.910
"How could we possibly piece
together enough evidence

01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:35.880
that would be highly
suggestive of something?"

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:38.580
So that's why we said, "All
right, obviously we can do

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:41.520
things cross-sectionally
and so let's think about,

01:01:41.520 --> 01:01:43.800
empirically, what are the types of things

01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:46.350
that can confound any kind of analysis,

01:01:46.350 --> 01:01:48.360
unobservable or observable."

01:01:48.360 --> 01:01:49.920
And that's sort of how we came up

01:01:49.920 --> 01:01:53.700
with the counterfactual towns

01:01:53.700 --> 01:01:55.260
in our cross-sectional analysis.

01:01:55.260 --> 01:01:57.630
And then we said, "Okay, how could we..."

01:01:57.630 --> 01:01:59.340
Essentially what we're trying to answer

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:01.290
is a long-term question,

01:02:01.290 --> 01:02:03.150
but we only have a snapshot of data.

01:02:03.150 --> 01:02:07.380
So how could we possibly
get data that would allow us

01:02:07.380 --> 01:02:11.010
to look at these longer
term effects, which,

01:02:11.010 --> 01:02:13.830
if you haven't worked with
the nighttime lights data,

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:15.450
I would encourage you to look into it.

01:02:15.450 --> 01:02:18.330
It's a really phenomenal set
of data and it allows you

01:02:18.330 --> 01:02:21.663
to answer questions even in
very remote parts of the world.

01:02:23.370 --> 01:02:26.640
So, yeah, I mean, I think we're
living in a fortunate time

01:02:26.640 --> 01:02:30.198
of data where there's
just all of this data

01:02:30.198 --> 01:02:32.970
that has become available to us.

01:02:32.970 --> 01:02:34.650
And that's both good and bad.

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:37.200
You have to be smart and intentional

01:02:37.200 --> 01:02:39.330
with how you use the data.

01:02:39.330 --> 01:02:41.730
But I hope that I've
convinced you that we were.

01:02:44.490 --> 01:02:45.390
- Yeah. Great.

01:02:45.390 --> 01:02:46.530
Are there other questions?

01:02:46.530 --> 01:02:48.403
I have a few more, if we....

01:02:51.390 --> 01:02:52.890
So if no one else has a question,

01:02:52.890 --> 01:02:56.070
I guess one thing I wanted
to in invite Sarah to do

01:02:56.070 --> 01:02:57.690
is talk about some of her current work.

01:02:57.690 --> 01:02:59.040
I know that's kind of evolved in sort

01:02:59.040 --> 01:03:00.480
of an econ history-ish direction,

01:03:00.480 --> 01:03:04.080
but also relating to migration
and also relating to,

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:06.540
I think, some of these
really important questions

01:03:06.540 --> 01:03:08.250
related to culture, institutions, history

01:03:08.250 --> 01:03:10.440
that kind of actually
get raised a little bit,

01:03:10.440 --> 01:03:13.470
especially in this sort of
longer term analysis that you do

01:03:13.470 --> 01:03:16.980
in this first paper.

01:03:16.980 --> 01:03:20.970
So I'd be curious to know
if you see any sort of

01:03:20.970 --> 01:03:23.610
through-line with this earlier
work to your current work,

01:03:23.610 --> 01:03:25.050
or you think of it as more of a departure?

01:03:25.050 --> 01:03:26.488
But it'd be great to hear about

01:03:26.488 --> 01:03:28.830
some of your current projects as well.

01:03:28.830 --> 01:03:29.730
- Yeah, I mean,

01:03:29.730 --> 01:03:31.440
I would love to talk
about my current projects

01:03:31.440 --> 01:03:32.940
and I'm super excited about them.

01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:37.740
I see them... So to back up
what Emily's referring to

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:40.770
is at the moment I'm
working on a large project

01:03:40.770 --> 01:03:43.770
that was funded by the
Australian Research Council

01:03:43.770 --> 01:03:48.000
to understand the dynamics
of racial violence

01:03:48.000 --> 01:03:52.380
in the United States, going
back to before the Civil War,

01:03:52.380 --> 01:03:55.380
all the way up until today.

01:03:55.380 --> 01:03:59.040
And obviously migration is a part of that.

01:03:59.040 --> 01:04:01.470
I'm not looking directly
at migration at the moment,

01:04:01.470 --> 01:04:05.670
but certainly conflict is
at the root of that project.

01:04:05.670 --> 01:04:10.260
And I do see these two as being related,

01:04:10.260 --> 01:04:13.710
certainly through the
long-term impacts that conflict

01:04:13.710 --> 01:04:17.400
can have on a society and
the protracted situations

01:04:17.400 --> 01:04:18.933
that it creates.

01:04:20.280 --> 01:04:24.150
And so what I'm doing in that
paper is essentially trying

01:04:24.150 --> 01:04:29.150
to understand how did
the Civil War contribute

01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:34.770
to protracted racial violence
in the United States.

01:04:34.770 --> 01:04:39.770
And so we Americans love
to talk about the Civil War

01:04:40.200 --> 01:04:41.970
and how it was the bloodiest war

01:04:41.970 --> 01:04:44.010
in all of our wars combined.

01:04:44.010 --> 01:04:47.610
And it was obviously a pivotal
moment for race relations

01:04:47.610 --> 01:04:48.870
in the United States.

01:04:48.870 --> 01:04:51.930
It ended the institution of slavery,

01:04:51.930 --> 01:04:55.140
and at least in the very
early years following,

01:04:55.140 --> 01:04:57.210
during reconstruction, ushered in an era

01:04:57.210 --> 01:05:01.020
of progressive civil rights
that were mainly granted

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:02.493
to Black men.

01:05:03.720 --> 01:05:08.550
And what I do in the paper
essentially is look at

01:05:08.550 --> 01:05:12.780
how did the casualties that
were sustained in the Civil War,

01:05:12.780 --> 01:05:17.780
so in the south, only white
men fought in the Confederacy.

01:05:19.080 --> 01:05:24.080
And nearly 25% of the
young male population,

01:05:24.960 --> 01:05:28.410
white male population, died
fighting in the Civil War.

01:05:28.410 --> 01:05:32.160
And that had a significant
demographic effect

01:05:32.160 --> 01:05:33.510
on the population.

01:05:33.510 --> 01:05:37.890
So not only do you have
a shift in the balance

01:05:37.890 --> 01:05:38.820
of racial power.

01:05:38.820 --> 01:05:42.750
Now there's relatively more
Black men than white men

01:05:42.750 --> 01:05:46.770
who now have political
rights and economic rights

01:05:46.770 --> 01:05:50.610
that are for some white
southerners perceived as a threat

01:05:50.610 --> 01:05:52.380
to the social order.

01:05:52.380 --> 01:05:57.060
But there's also a shift in
gendered power because you have

01:05:57.060 --> 01:06:00.000
all these young white men who are dying,

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:03.270
and now there's a what we'd
call engrossed econ speed.

01:06:03.270 --> 01:06:06.600
We would say there's a
surplus of white women,

01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:08.970
or there's this disruption
to what we would call

01:06:08.970 --> 01:06:10.320
the white marriage market.

01:06:10.320 --> 01:06:13.380
So there's a generation
of young white women

01:06:13.380 --> 01:06:15.600
who are unmarried.

01:06:15.600 --> 01:06:18.540
They're either widowed or
they can't find partners

01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:21.750
who are their age because
they have died in the war.

01:06:21.750 --> 01:06:25.200
And so essentially what I'm
looking at is the intersection

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:27.090
of these two forms of power.

01:06:27.090 --> 01:06:30.030
So you have a shift in racial power

01:06:30.030 --> 01:06:32.220
and you have a shift in gendered power,

01:06:32.220 --> 01:06:37.220
and that creates a perfect
storm of racism and violence.

01:06:38.220 --> 01:06:42.330
And what we're finding
in this paper is that,

01:06:42.330 --> 01:06:45.240
not only is there an
increase in racial violence

01:06:45.240 --> 01:06:50.240
in places where the white
male death rate was higher,

01:06:52.770 --> 01:06:55.530
but there's a particular type of violence.

01:06:55.530 --> 01:06:59.760
So lynchings were the
main form of violence

01:06:59.760 --> 01:07:04.760
used against Black people
for a hundred-plus years.

01:07:05.850 --> 01:07:10.850
But in particular in
about 40% of lynchings,

01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:14.190
when you look at the data,

01:07:14.190 --> 01:07:18.360
the excuse given for the
lynching was that a Black man

01:07:18.360 --> 01:07:20.553
had sexually assaulted a white woman.

01:07:21.540 --> 01:07:24.630
And we know Ida B. Welles was
working on this in the 1890s.

01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:27.780
We know that these are false accusations.

01:07:27.780 --> 01:07:30.450
And she has a really
famous quote saying that

01:07:30.450 --> 01:07:33.210
if you look at four-fifths
of these accusations,

01:07:33.210 --> 01:07:35.070
they would be considered adultery.

01:07:35.070 --> 01:07:37.050
And so what she's
insinuating is that actually

01:07:37.050 --> 01:07:39.690
these are consensual
relationships that were happening

01:07:39.690 --> 01:07:41.640
between white women and Black men.

01:07:41.640 --> 01:07:46.640
And because they were
taboo and bucked the norms

01:07:47.130 --> 01:07:51.900
of the social order, they
were often called rape.

01:07:51.900 --> 01:07:55.080
And someone's father finds
out that his daughter

01:07:55.080 --> 01:07:57.990
is having an illicit
relationship and tells her

01:07:57.990 --> 01:07:59.520
to claim that she's been raped.

01:07:59.520 --> 01:08:01.260
And this justifies the lynchings.

01:08:01.260 --> 01:08:04.510
And in fact, "To Kill a
Mockingbird" is all about this.

01:08:04.510 --> 01:08:09.510
So this is an enduring
phenomenon in our culture.

01:08:10.590 --> 01:08:13.680
And what our work is essentially showing

01:08:13.680 --> 01:08:16.530
is that in these places
where more white men died

01:08:16.530 --> 01:08:19.830
in the Civil War, you see an
increase in racial violence,

01:08:19.830 --> 01:08:23.100
but it's primarily through
this sexual channel.

01:08:23.100 --> 01:08:27.420
So you only see more lynchings
using sexual excuses.

01:08:27.420 --> 01:08:30.510
You don't see more lynchings
for murder or assault

01:08:30.510 --> 01:08:34.500
or arson or theft, it's only
using the sexual excuse.

01:08:34.500 --> 01:08:38.460
And then we combine that
with data from the newspapers

01:08:38.460 --> 01:08:41.850
that we scraped keywords from newspapers,

01:08:41.850 --> 01:08:45.180
which were highly localized at the time.

01:08:45.180 --> 01:08:48.810
And what we find is there's
a narrative in the places

01:08:48.810 --> 01:08:53.550
where more white deaths
happened in the Civil War.

01:08:53.550 --> 01:08:55.530
There's a narrative in the newspapers

01:08:55.530 --> 01:08:58.890
that Black men are rapists, essentially.

01:08:58.890 --> 01:09:02.820
And we actually don't see
this during reconstruction.

01:09:02.820 --> 01:09:05.580
So we have a very long-term set of data,

01:09:05.580 --> 01:09:08.940
we're looking from 1850
all the way up until today.

01:09:08.940 --> 01:09:12.030
And you don't see this narrative happening

01:09:12.030 --> 01:09:13.470
during reconstruction,

01:09:13.470 --> 01:09:17.370
when the south was
occupied by federal troops.

01:09:17.370 --> 01:09:19.200
But as soon as reconstruction ends

01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:20.700
and as soon as the Democrats,

01:09:20.700 --> 01:09:23.820
which were not the
progressive party at the time,

01:09:23.820 --> 01:09:25.800
are able to run again for office,

01:09:25.800 --> 01:09:28.680
you see more support for
the Democratic party.

01:09:28.680 --> 01:09:32.220
and you also see the
votes shift Republican

01:09:32.220 --> 01:09:33.660
in the late fifties and sixties,

01:09:33.660 --> 01:09:36.660
when the parties shifted
their policies toward race.

01:09:36.660 --> 01:09:40.320
You see this narrative
being used in the newspapers

01:09:40.320 --> 01:09:44.220
that's consistent all
the way up until 1930,

01:09:44.220 --> 01:09:46.080
which is where our data ends for that.

01:09:46.080 --> 01:09:49.860
And then when you look at the
implicit association test,

01:09:49.860 --> 01:09:52.020
which I don't know how
familiar any of you guys are

01:09:52.020 --> 01:09:54.210
with this, but it's a test
that Harvard does online.

01:09:54.210 --> 01:09:55.200
You should take it.

01:09:55.200 --> 01:09:58.620
And it gives you pictures
of Black and white faces

01:09:58.620 --> 01:10:01.382
and good and bad words, and
you associate these words

01:10:01.382 --> 01:10:04.530
with faces.

01:10:04.530 --> 01:10:08.160
We find actually no
effect on implicit bias.

01:10:08.160 --> 01:10:11.130
But when you look at the
self-reported questions

01:10:11.130 --> 01:10:14.610
that respondents are asked
at the end of the survey,

01:10:14.610 --> 01:10:18.540
there is an association
today among respondents

01:10:18.540 --> 01:10:21.210
who are living in a place
that had a big shock

01:10:21.210 --> 01:10:24.120
during the Civil War and
their racial attitudes.

01:10:24.120 --> 01:10:27.330
And they're largely
ones that are consistent

01:10:27.330 --> 01:10:28.163
with this narrative.

01:10:28.163 --> 01:10:29.370
So they're more likely to think

01:10:29.370 --> 01:10:30.960
that Black people are violent.

01:10:30.960 --> 01:10:34.470
They're less likely to want
to have a sexual relationship

01:10:34.470 --> 01:10:35.550
with a Black person.

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:40.380
They're less likely to want
to have a Black roommate.

01:10:40.380 --> 01:10:44.910
They feel nervous and
uncomfortable around Black people.

01:10:44.910 --> 01:10:47.340
So yeah, this is still a work in progress.

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:50.850
And we have a working paper
that's up on my website,

01:10:50.850 --> 01:10:52.110
if you'd like to read it.

01:10:52.110 --> 01:10:53.880
We're in the process of revising it.

01:10:53.880 --> 01:10:58.880
But yeah, it's an obvious tragic finding,

01:11:00.660 --> 01:11:03.810
but I think it's an important
one for us to share,

01:11:03.810 --> 01:11:05.960
and we're excited to
be able to share that.

01:11:07.980 --> 01:11:10.380
- Absolutely. I'll
definitely check it out.

01:11:10.380 --> 01:11:13.590
And I think, strangely, kind
of there is a connection

01:11:13.590 --> 01:11:15.120
with this sort of legacies of conflict,

01:11:15.120 --> 01:11:18.120
legacies of violence literature.

01:11:18.120 --> 01:11:18.953
- Yeah.

01:11:18.953 --> 01:11:23.648
- Yes. Any other questions on that case?

01:11:23.648 --> 01:11:26.730
Let me thank again, Sarah, especially,

01:11:26.730 --> 01:11:28.290
for being so flexible at the time,

01:11:28.290 --> 01:11:30.030
so we can all make it.

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:32.340
A large number of students I
know are also gonna watch this

01:11:32.340 --> 01:11:35.430
on recording, so I'd also like
to thank you for additionally

01:11:35.430 --> 01:11:37.470
to this great presentation
to being so candid

01:11:37.470 --> 01:11:39.690
about sort of the experience
of working in this topic.

01:11:39.690 --> 01:11:41.340
I think to me is really
extraordinarily valuable

01:11:41.340 --> 01:11:42.450
for people to hear.

01:11:42.450 --> 01:11:45.150
And we're looking forward to seeing

01:11:45.150 --> 01:11:47.313
how this new research agenda develops.

01:11:48.240 --> 01:11:49.317
- Great. Thank you so much, Emily.

01:11:49.317 --> 01:11:51.420
And thank you so much to
the center for having me.

01:11:51.420 --> 01:11:52.470
This has really been wonderful.

01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:54.540
And please, for those
of you who are watching

01:11:54.540 --> 01:11:56.610
the recorded version or even watching now,

01:11:56.610 --> 01:11:58.350
really feel free to reach out.

01:11:58.350 --> 01:12:00.690
I'm always happy to talk about my research

01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:02.290
and would love to hear from you.

01:12:03.540 --> 01:12:06.490
- [Emily] Thank you very much.
A pleasure to have you here.

